# Recorded from the published vocabulary rather than from a second runtime;
# see docs/fixture-provenance.md for where to obtain the model. No mandatory
# test reads this file.
#
# What it pins is the one thing a synthetic fixture cannot: the convention a
# converted WordPiece vocabulary is actually written in. The architecture's
# papers say a word starts bare and a continuation carries two leading
# hashes. A GGUF vocabulary says the reverse -- a word's first piece carries
# a leading U+2581 and a continuation is bare -- and of the 30,522 pieces in
# this file not one begins with the hashes while 23,695 begin with the
# marker.
#
# Written from the paper, the engine spelled every word out of continuation
# pieces: "a" found the bare "a" that continues a word rather than the
# marked one that starts it. Nothing refused, and a real model embedded text
# nobody wrote -- an unrelated pair of sentences scored 0.479 where it
# scores 0.007 now. The fixture had invented the hashes, so the engine and
# the reader written independently against the same description agreed with
# each other about a model nobody ships.
#
# The identifiers below are bert-base-uncased's, which all-MiniLM-L6-v2
# carries unchanged and which are published: "unaffable" is the canonical
# WordPiece example and spells as una + ##ff + ##able.

runtime published vocabulary, bert-base-uncased
model all-MiniLM-L6-v2-Q8_0.gguf

# One prompt, because a recording describes one. "unaffable" is the canonical
# WordPiece example and the one that says most: it is spelled from a piece
# that starts a word and two that continue it, so a reader with the marker
# convention backwards cannot produce these three identifiers by accident.
# The other two checked by hand were "a test sentence" -> 1037 3231 6251 and
# "Hello, World!" -> 7592 1010 2088 999.

prompt unaffable
tokens 14477 20961 3468
